## Work2 Boundary

Work2 includes the bounded Windows-target preflight, recovery proof, latest-release provenance,
transactional field upgrade, focused changed-behavior checks, a scoped local commit when source
changes exist, deployment to the explicitly verified Windows work environment, release-cohort
registration for any canonical candidate, governed checkpoint/rebuild proof, and strict Doctor in
the applicable project.

Work2 does not deploy the `ts.rookaro.com` documentation site for this outcome, push Git, publish a
release, or cross into an unverified project identity. A defect repair may reach a registered
Work2 candidate but cannot be used as a new stable target input until Work5 releases it.

## Authority and Stop Conditions

- The current project identity cannot mutate the external Windows workspace.
- Stop before remote mutation until `workpc` and the exact path are verified under an explicit
  target switch and fresh target-local instructions, Tool Shed skill, identity, and state.
- Stop or roll back on host/path mismatch, unexpected links or junctions, inadequate space, failed
  provenance, unproven recovery, unrelated Git damage, storage-mode change, or failed qualification.
- Credentials and authentication changes remain explicit operator boundaries.

## Verification Strategy

- Windows-native commands verify paths, tool versions, Git state, manifest/tag/content provenance,
  disk and link safety, backup hashes, rollback, updater behavior, version, identity, storage mode,
  installed skill, and firmware-work preservation.
- Linux and Windows focused tests qualify only an actually reproduced canonical repair.
- Evidence remains sanitized and retains recovery assets without copying firmware content into this
  repository.

